---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Showcase from "../../components/Showcase.astro";
import StylingHooks from "../../components/StylingHooks.astro";

const import_ = `@import "@skryensya/core/patterns/scrollbar.css";`;

const always = `<div
  class="sk-scrollbar demo-scroll"
  tabindex="0"
>
  <p>
    Region with native scroll and system chrome: track and thumb always visible. The wheel, momentum and touch remain from the platform; The pattern only paints the surface that the browser exposes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Adds <code>sk-scrollbar</code> to any container with <code>overflow: auto</code> (or <code>scroll</code>). Without the class, the OS paints its native scrollbar.
  </p>
  <p>
    Third paragraph to force overflow and view the thumb. Change mode or contrast above: hooks follow semantic tokens.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fourth paragraph. The size of the thumb is decided by the browser depending on how much content there is; The system only sets the width of the rail and the colors.
  </p>
</div>`;

const reveal = `<div
  class="sk-scrollbar sk-scrollbar--reveal demo-scroll"
  tabindex="0"
>
  <p>
    Same region, variant <code>sk-scrollbar--reveal</code>: the gutter is reserved (<code>scrollbar-gutter: stable</code>) but the thumb is transparent until you hover or keyboard focus enters the region.
  </p>
  <p>
    When revealing, a filled track does not appear: only the thumb, where you are. A painted rail would read as a border that the layout did not call for.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mouse over here, or tab to this box, to see the thumb. When exiting, it remains silent again without moving the layout.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ideal in dense sidebars and rails, where an always-on scrollbar competes with content.
  </p>
</div>`;
---

<Base
  title="Scrollbar"
  description="Opt-in pattern that paints the browser's native scrollbar: always visible or quiet until hover and focus."
>
  <h1>Scrollbar</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Does not replace the native scroll. Colors the surface that the browser already exposes (<code>scrollbar-color</code>, <code>scrollbar-width</code> and the <code>::-webkit-scrollbar*</code> pseudo-elements). The wheel, momentum, touch pad, and OS preferences remain platform-specific.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it is a pattern</h2>
  <p>
    Several hosts share the same rail: the docs sidebar, the drawer, the customize canvas. Sending the paint once prevents each surface from reinventing a thumb. It is <strong>opt-in</strong>, like nav-list: an app can keep the OS scrollbar.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={import_} label="CSS" />
  <p>
    Then mark the region that is already scrolling (<code>overflow: auto</code> or <code>scroll</code>, with a limited size). The pattern does not define the overflow: it only paints the scrollbar that the browser exposes.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`<!-- your container with overflow: auto -->
<div class="sk-scrollbar">…</div>

<!-- o la variante silenciosa hasta hover/foco -->
<div class="sk-scrollbar sk-scrollbar--reveal">…</div>`}
    label="HTML"
  />

  <h2>Always visible</h2>
  <p>
    <code>sk-scrollbar</code> leaves track and thumb visible. Use it when the scroll is part of the UI map (a code panel, a wide table).
  </p>
  <Showcase html={always} label="sk-scrollbar" />

  <h2>Reveal on hover (and focus)</h2>
  <p>
    <code>sk-scrollbar sk-scrollbar--reveal</code> reserves the gutter so as not to move the layout, and leaves the thumb transparent until <code>: hover</code> or <code>: focus-within</code>. When revealed, only the thumb appears, not a filled track.
  </p>
  <Showcase html={reveal} label="sk-scrollbar--reveal" note="hover or tab to reveal" />
  <p>
    The sidebar rail in this documentation uses exactly this variant: still at rest, readable when you touch it.
  </p>

  <h2>Which one to choose</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Always visible</strong> when the reader needs to know in advance that there is more content (long lists, logs, editors).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reveal</strong> when chrome competes with content (navigation, touch, narrow panels). The stable gutter prevents CLS when the thumb appears.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Freezing the scroll of the <em>document</em> behind a modal is another pattern: <a class="sk-link sk-interactive" href="/en/scroll-lock">scroll lock</a>. They do not step on each other: one paints regions, the other freezes <code>html</code>.
  </p>

  <StylingHooks
    groups={[{ file: "patterns/scrollbar.css", probe: "sk-scrollbar" }]}
  />

  <h2>Contract</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Opt-in: Import <code>patterns/scrollbar.css</code> and mark the region with <code>sk-scrollbar</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The region must be scrollable (<code>overflow: auto|scroll</code> and a limited size). The pattern does not define the overflow.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>sk-scrollbar--reveal</code> also requires <code>sk-scrollbar</code> (the variant only re-declares hooks; the base provides the rest).
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not invent a thumb in JS or hide the native scroll with a fake div. If the OS uses scrollbar overlay, the reveal gutter is a no-op and does not bother.
    </li>
    <li>
      The <code>--sk-scrollbar-*</code> hooks are stepped on from your CSS on <code>.sk-scrollbar</code>, just like any other styling hook.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Demo rails: enough height to overflow, so the thumb is real and not a theoretical claim. */
  :global(.demo-scroll) {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    block-size: 11rem;
    overflow: auto;
    padding: var(--space-inset-md);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-surface);
    background: var(--color-bg-surface);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  :global(.demo-scroll) p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  :global(.demo-scroll) p + p {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-stack-sm);
  }

  :global(.demo-scroll) code {
    font-size: 0.92em;
  }
</style>
